  <main class="hero">
    <video autoplay muted loop playsinline>
      <source src="video.mp4" type="video/mp4">
    </video>
    <h1>Explore Zeroh</h1>
    <button class="waitlist-button">Join Waitlist</button>
  </main>
</body>
</html>
```

### Evaluation

Both answers provide usable HTML/CSS for a centered hero, background video, and a button that changes to white with black text on hover. Both also disable scrolling.

The main omission in **both** answers is the requested faded appearance of the button before hover: neither applies reduced opacity or otherwise makes the button look faded. Assistant A’s button is simply white text with a white border on a transparent background. Assistant B dims the video and adds an overlay, but does not fade the button itself. B also makes the button label uppercase, which differs slightly from the wording requested.

**Assistant B is slightly better.** Its video uses fixed viewport dimensions and `object-fit: cover`, and it includes responsive heading sizing and a contrast overlay. Those are useful refinements, though the external video URL creates a dependency and is less reliable than a local asset. Assistant A is simpler and more direct, but its video sizing is less robust and it likewise requires a video file to be supplied. Neither fully satisfies the button’s faded initial state.

My final verdict is: [[B>A]]

A suitable implementation would keep the video, overlay, and text in separate visible layers, prevent page scrolling, and make the outlined button slightly translucent until hovered:

