## My answer to the prompt

A **Strategy pattern** can work well here, with an `AccountType` and an account ID. But the important point is that an ID by itself does not identify which table to search: IDs may overlap across the two tables, and separate repositories cannot reliably infer the entity type. Prefer an API that receives both the type and ID.

For example:

```java
public enum AccountType {
    USER, DOCTOR
}

public record AccountView(Integer id, String phone, String fullName) {}
```

Each strategy handles one account type:

```java
public interface AccountLookupStrategy {
    AccountType type();
    AccountView findById(Integer id);
}
```

```java
public interface UserAccountRepository
        extends JpaRepository<UserAccount, Integer> {}

public interface DoctorAccountRepository
        extends JpaRepository<DoctorAccount, Integer> {}
```

```java
@Service
public class UserAccountLookup implements AccountLookupStrategy {
    private final UserAccountRepository repository;

    public UserAccountLookup(UserAccountRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    @Override
    public AccountType type() {
        return AccountType.USER;
    }

    @Override
    public AccountView findById(Integer id) {
        UserAccount account = repository.findById(id)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new NoSuchElementException("User account not found: " + id));
        return new AccountView(account.getId(), account.getPhone(), account.getFullName());
    }
}
```

```java
@Service
public class DoctorAccountLookup implements AccountLookupStrategy {
    private final DoctorAccountRepository repository;

    public DoctorAccountLookup(DoctorAccountRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    @Override
    public AccountType type() {
        return AccountType.DOCTOR;
    }

    @Override
    public AccountView findById(Integer id) {
        DoctorAccount account = repository.findById(id)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new NoSuchElementException("Doctor account not found: " + id));
        return new AccountView(account.getId(), account.getPhone(), account.getFullName());
    }
}
```

The service can register all strategies without a switch statement:

```java
@Service
public class AccountService {
    private final Map<AccountType, AccountLookupStrategy> lookups;

    public AccountService(List<AccountLookupStrategy> strategies) {
        this.lookups = strategies.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.toMap(AccountLookupStrategy::type, Function.identity()));
    }

    public AccountView find(AccountType type, Integer id) {
        AccountLookupStrategy lookup = lookups.get(type);
        if (lookup == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unsupported account type: " + type);
        }
        return lookup.findById(id);
    }
}
```

For a new account type, add its entity, repository, and strategy. If callers must provide only an ID, you need a separate way to determine the type—for example, a globally unique ID registry. Searching every repository is possible, but is fragile if IDs can overlap.

## Evaluation

